\f0\fs40 \cf2 Corneal cross-linking is a treatment used to strengthen the cornea, most often in people with progressive keratoconus or other forms of corneal weakening. The goal is usually not to make vision perfect, but to slow or stop further worsening of the corneal shape.\
\
In the standard treatment, the surface layer of the cornea is removed, vitamin B2 drops are placed on the eye, and then a controlled ultraviolet light is applied. This creates additional bonds within the corneal tissue, making it stronger and more stable. Some newer approaches may leave the surface layer in place, but not every patient is a suitable candidate for that method.\
\
After the procedure, the eye is usually uncomfortable for several days while the surface heals. Light sensitivity, tearing, foreign body sensation, and blurred vision are common early on. A bandage contact lens is often used temporarily, and prescription drops are usually needed during healing.\
\
Vision is often blurry at first and may fluctuate for weeks. Improvement is gradual, and the main purpose is stabilization. Some patients later need updated glasses, contact lenses, or additional treatments to achieve the best possible vision.\
\
Risks can include infection, delayed healing, corneal haze, scarring, sterile infiltrates, worsening pain, or, rarely, reduced vision. Contact the clinic promptly if pain becomes severe, redness is increasing, or vision suddenly worsens.\
\
Regular follow-up is important to monitor healing and confirm that the cornea has stabilized over time.}